  while swapping [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m for something else touches only [38;5;153msession.js[39m — the two changes you've already said are
  coming.
  [3mComplexity:[0m moderate.

  [1mB. Observable identity store[0m
  Same split, but [38;5;153msession.js[39m becomes a pub/sub store: [38;5;153msubscribe(fn)[39m, and consumers re-render when identity appears or
  clears.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m buys automatic UI reaction across many forms; costs listener registration, teardown, and a class of subtle
  leak bugs. [3mWhen it wins:[0m several always-mounted views must visibly react to login/logout. You have one form and no
  logout.
  [3mComplexity:[0m high. [1mI'd skip it[0m — this is machinery for a problem you don't have yet, and on-demand reads convert to a
  store later without touching callers.

  [1mC. Storage-only, no module state[0m
  No layering and no in-memory copy: one tiny [38;5;153msession.js[39m with [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m/[38;5;153msetUserId()[39m reading and writing [38;5;153msessionStorage
[39m  directly, and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m writes the id itself.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m fewest moving parts, and no possibility of an in-memory copy drifting from storage. But [38;5;153mlogin()[39m then does
  two jobs — authenticate [3mand[0m persist — which is exactly the seam you'll need to cut when real auth arrives, and it
  JSON-parses on every read. [3mWhen it wins:[0m if this stays a one-form toy forever.
  [3mComplexity:[0m trivial.

  [1mWhy A:[0m your two stated futures are "real auth later" and "other forms later." A is the only one of the three that puts
  a seam at each of those points without inventing subscriber infrastructure for a single form.
